---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/alternatives/recart-alternative";
const title = "Recart alternatives for Shopify: SMS, recovery and service handover";
const desc = "Compare retaining Recart, changing SMS providers or consolidating channels. Hand over capture, numbers, replies, cart and browse queues and managed-service work.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Recart just a Messenger recovery tool?",
    "answer": "No. Its current site presents Shopify SMS, OneClick opt-in, campaigns, automation, cart and browse recovery and client strategy."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a number move with the subscriber export?",
    "answer": "A contact export does not port a number. Confirm number ownership, eligibility and routing with both suppliers and test inbound replies and opt-outs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Messenger consent become SMS consent?",
    "answer": "No. Preserve channel-specific evidence and review eligibility. Do not infer permission for email or SMS from a legacy Messenger relationship."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens to waiting cart messages?",
    "answer": "Record remaining actions and choose completion, cancellation or reconstruction. Recheck purchases and opt-outs before dispatch and prevent duplicate enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "What replaces Recart strategy support?",
    "answer": "Assign campaign, creative, scheduling, recovery, reply and reporting work to named people or a contracted service and include that cost."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy a direct replacement?",
    "answer": "Its SaaS email positioning can support an email evaluation but does not establish SMS, number, reply or Shopify recovery parity. Demonstrate the required integration first."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Migration decision guide</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Evaluate today's SMS program and managed work</h2>
<p><a href="https://recart.com/">Recart currently presents Shopify SMS, OneClick opt-in, list growth, campaigns, automation, cart and browse abandonment, integrations and client strategy</a>. Its present-day decision is not adequately described as a dead Messenger recovery tool. Keep it on the shortlist when acquisition, recovery and strategy support fit the team's needs. Verify your contracted services and account controls rather than interpreting marketing claims as measured store results.</p>
<p>Repair event quality or offer strategy if that solves the actual problem. Change the SMS supplier when a demonstrated destination, control, integration, service or contract need is unmet. Consolidate channels only after testing the proposed account's coordination. Intentionally retiring a channel is another decision and requires a customer and support plan; legacy Messenger permission is not permission to email or text.</p>
<p>Investigate Postscript or Attentive for the SMS and service requirements you specify; Klaviyo or Omnisend for a proposed consolidated account; Rejoiner for an email recovery requirement. These are candidates to demonstrate, not a league table. Privy warrants evaluation for its advertised capture, email and SMS scope. <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">Sequenzy's SaaS email and automation positioning</a> does not establish SMS, number or Shopify recovery parity. <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS reports decommissioning</a>, so SMSBump is not a new SMS destination; Reviews and Loyalty have separate scope.</p>
<h2>Replace the service as well as the software</h2>
<p>Write down what Recart staff, your agency and your internal team actually do: popup edits, campaign planning, copy, audience selection, scheduling, recovery optimization, reply triage, reporting and billing reconciliation. Identify the named person who will do each task after exit, their access and response expectations. A self-service account with similar checkboxes can leave a large operating gap if strategy and execution previously arrived as a service.</p>
<p>Request campaign calendars, approved templates, exclusion logic, experiment notes and unresolved support work before access ends. Record who approves offers, who can publish and who handles nights or weekends. Test a rejected creative and a last-minute stock change through that workflow. Include the new labor or replacement managed-service fee in the business case; do not assume software consolidation eliminates it.</p>
<h2>Preserve opt-in routes and number ownership</h2>
<p>Inventory the OneClick or other popups actually used, checkout capture, links, keywords and physical placements. Record destination, displayed disclosure, offer and source ID for each. Reproduce the shopper experience on mobile and desktop, including a returning subscriber, declined SMS choice and an interrupted signup. A click or email collected by a form must not silently become text eligibility in the destination.</p>
<p>For each sending number, obtain its type, ownership authorization, supported destinations, port eligibility and expected handoff steps from both providers. Decide whether to port or introduce a new number and how existing links, keywords and customer replies will behave. Keep credentials private. Do not cancel the old account before its release and routing dependencies are settled. A scheduled port date is not evidence that inbound replies or opt-outs already reach the new account.</p>
<h2>Replies need a working destination</h2>
<p>Document inbound routing, opt-out handling, help responses and escalation to a human. Preserve open conversations and service context through an approved export or handover record where available. Test an ordinary product question, a shipping complaint, an opt-out and a reply to an old campaign after cutover. The support owner should know which inbox receives each case and how to find the original offer.</p>
<p>When a new number is necessary, explain sender identity through an eligible communication route and update live capture references. Do not mass-message an old Messenger audience to request SMS permission unless that communication is itself permitted and technically supported. Rollback after a port may need another provider/carrier process; re-enabling an old flow cannot restore number routing. Keep an alternative support contact available during the handoff.</p>
<h2>Cart and browse recovery have different identity tests</h2>
<p>For cart recovery, document the qualifying event, cart link, delay, offer, purchase cancellation, expiry and allowed channel. Test a shopper purchasing while a message waits, a cart edited on another device, a refund after purchase and a stale checkout link. Specify whether a refund should ever create new eligibility; importing order history must not automatically replay abandonment. At dispatch, recheck purchase status and suppression rather than relying only on enrollment-time data.</p>
<p>For browse recovery, demonstrate how a viewed product becomes associated with an eligible identifiable subscriber. An anonymous page view is not a textable profile. Test an out-of-stock variant, a price change, consent withdrawn while waiting and multiple product views that could otherwise create competing messages. Record the decision on frequency and priority between browse, cart and campaigns so one shopper does not receive conflicting offers.</p>
<p>For every queued message, record journey version, last action, next due time and offer state where accessible. Choose old-provider completion, cancellation or deliberate reconstruction of eligible remaining work. Keep new events from enrolling in both suppliers. A test event with a stable ID should be traceable from Shopify through eligibility to one sender; retry it to check duplicate handling. Reconcile waiting counts before releasing the new queue.</p>
<h2>Integration and cost reconciliation</h2>
<p>Inspect theme embeds, checkout extensions, subscription apps, helpdesk, loyalty and existing email automations for Recart-specific destinations. Assign event and suppression writers; define retry behavior and alert ownership. Validate any email/SMS coordination in the intended accounts. Moving cart SMS does not require moving a working email program, and adding welcome or replenishment should follow an actual requirement rather than an assumed graduation path.</p>
<p>Request comparable quotes including subscription, messaging usage, carrier or number charges, destination markets, support, minimum commitments, agency labor and overlapping accounts. Use forecast message counts and message composition with each supplier's billing rules. Include exit work and service replacement. There is no universal monthly-order level at which migration becomes economical.</p>
<p>Hypothetical recovery arithmetic: one additional $120 order per day over 30 days is $3,600 gross revenue, not $3,600 profit or proof of lift. At an assumed 40% contribution before messaging it contributes $1,440; subtract $300 incremental messaging and $400 operating labor to leave $740 before migration cost. A $2,960 migration would then take four months to recover only if those orders are truly incremental. A holdout, returns and overlap can change that conclusion.</p><h2>Export permission and exclusions before changing writers</h2>
<p>Keep the original export read-only with its date and field dictionary. Reconcile total records, unique channel identifiers, duplicates, unsubscribes, complaints and records held for review. Preserve available signup source, disclosure version, timestamp, channel status and suppression reason; do not turn a blank field into permission. An email address and a telephone number on one profile are separate messaging decisions. Review eligibility for the intended destinations with the responsible team. Import acceptance proves only that a file loaded.</p>
<p>Test opt-out after export but before import, a repeat submission from an unsubscribed contact, and conflicting status from another integration. Record which system wins and how quickly suppression reaches every active sender. Keep exclusions even when removing inactive profiles from billable audiences. Verify mobile unsubscribe and preference links before activation. Choose sending volume from actual reputation, audience and provider guidance rather than a universal day-by-day ramp.</p>
<h2>Reporting and a reversible stopping point</h2>
<p>Archive campaign identifiers, attribution windows, timezone, UTM rules, refunds and flow-level send counts before cancellation. Compare eligible customers, messages delivered, opt-outs, purchases and contribution under consistent definitions. Attributed revenue is not incremental profit: an order credited to two tools is still one order. Where feasible use a holdout or comparable cohorts; document seasonal and offer differences when that comparison is weak.</p>
<p>Name the person who can stop new enrollments and queued sends in each tool. Rollback starts by stopping the faulty writer, retaining new opt-outs and reconciling already-sent messages before restoring any old automation. Do not replay historical orders or every imported subscriber into welcome. Keep access and exports for the agreed retention period, then remove obsolete scripts, credentials and agency access after dependencies have been checked. Cancellation is a separate billing action from pausing a flow.</p>
<h2>Related guides</h2><p><a class="link" href="/compare/recart-vs-postscript">recart vs postscript</a> · <a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives</a> · <a class="link" href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business">can spam act compliance guide business</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump">postscript vs smsbump</a> · <a class="link" href="/apps/recart">recart</a> · <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned cart</a> · <a class="link" href="/alternatives/rejoiner-alternative">rejoiner alternative</a></p>
<p>Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026. The rehearsals above are proposed checks, not measured merchant results or tested account parity.</p>
</article></div></section>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Recart alternatives FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
